<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 1650–1800 (the viceroyalties of New Spain and Peru)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Viceroy to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/potosi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potosi.jpg" alt="Potosí"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a><span class="soc-credit">Parallelepiped09 · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Spanish law divided the people of its American kingdoms into two “republics”: the república de españoles, which came to include everyone of African or mixed descent as well as the Spaniards, and the república de indios, the Indigenous towns with their own nobles, councils and duties. Within them, colonial law and ideology ranked people by descent and colour, alongside wealth, office and legitimacy. Spaniards paid no tribute and could hold office, study and be ordained; Indigenous commoners paid tribute and owed labour drafts; free people of African descent paid tribute too and bore the stigma colonial ideology attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The casta paintings of the 18th century show a neat ladder of mixtures, but many historians now see a looser order, in which a person’s standing (calidad) turned on wealth, occupation and reputation as well as birth, and could change from one record to the next.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 1650–1800 (the viceroyalties of New Spain and Peru)</span><span><b>People</b> 6.5–7 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 9</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>, <a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, <a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The viceroy</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:37%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Spanish-born Spaniards</span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.2–0.3%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:49%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:20%"><span class="soc-bar-n">American-born Spaniards</span><span class="soc-bar-s">18–22%</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The clergy</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:62%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Indigenous nobles</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:75%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:13%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Mestizos</span><span class="soc-bar-s">11–15%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:87%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:57%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Indigenous commoners</span><span class="soc-bar-s">At most 57% (the rest)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:10%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Free people of African descent</span><span class="soc-bar-s">10%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.2%</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-de-amat-y-junyent" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/manuel-de-amat-y-junyent.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The viceroy <span class="soc-term">virrey</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The viceroy ruled New Spain or Peru for the king of Spain, who never crossed the ocean to visit.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">No Spanish king ever visited his American kingdoms. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> he was represented by a viceroy (virrey, “vice-king”), appointed for a fixed term and almost always a Spanish-born nobleman, soldier or churchman: in the 17th century several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and archbishops served as viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. As captain-general he commanded the armed forces of the provinces under him; as vice-patron he oversaw the Church and the Inquisition; he appointed the district magistrates (corregidores) and answered for good government, economic development and the humane treatment of the Indigenous population. A court gathered around him and the vicereine. His power had limits. The judges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a>, the high court, could write to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> without his leave, and at the end of his term every viceroy faced a residencia, a public inquiry into his conduct. From the 1780s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> set intendants beside him, whose fiscal powers cut into his authority.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The king chose viceroys from the Spanish nobility, the army and the higher clergy, often after service elsewhere in the empire: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-de-amat-y-junyent">Manuel de Amat y Junyent</a> governed Chile before he became viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1761. The post ended with recall, death or the end of the term.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man in each viceroyalty, with his household and court; the king himself remained in Spain.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-de-amat-y-junyent">Manuel de Amat y Junyent</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-manso-de-velasco">José Antonio Manso de Velasco</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teodoro-de-croix">Teodoro de Croix</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-galvez">Bernardo de Gálvez</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jose-de-galvez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>0.2–0.3%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Spanish-born Spaniards <span class="soc-term">peninsulares; gachupines (Mexico), chapetones (Peru)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Spaniards born in Spain held most of the top jobs in the colonies, as judges, bishops and royal officials.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Spaniards born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the peninsulares, were a small minority who held most of the highest offices. The viceroys, most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> judges and other royal officials, among them the intendants of the 1780s, came from Spain, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> the crown preferred Spanish-born men for the bishoprics too. The great overseas trade was run by family firms based in Spain, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining lay largely with Spanish-born mine owners and their merchant backers. In law a peninsular was simply a Spaniard; the difference lay in office and the crown’s trust. Many arrived young and poor, as clerks in a kinsman’s business, and married into American-born families, so that their children were criollos. American-born Spaniards called them gachupines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and chapetones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and resented them most when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> gave them offices that criollos had held: in the late 17th century, when the crown sold seats on the Audiencias, American-born men had held 45 per cent of them.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A Spaniard became a peninsular by sailing to the Indies with the crown’s licence, meant only for Catholics who could show they had no Jewish or Muslim ancestors. His children born in America were criollos.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Roughly: independent Mexico expelled some 10,000–20,000 Spanish-born residents in the 1820s and 1830s, about 0.2–0.3 per cent of its people; earlier figures are uncertain.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez">José de Gálvez</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-escandon">José de Escandón</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-mexico">Real Audiencia of Mexico</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-lima">Real Audiencia of Lima</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/agustin-de-iturbide.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>18–22%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">American-born Spaniards <span class="soc-term">criollos, españoles americanos</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Criollos were people of Spanish families born in America; some were rich landowners and many were craftsmen or clerks.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Spaniards born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the criollos, formed the largest privileged group. In law they were españoles: they paid no tribute and could hold office, enter the universities and be ordained. Their elite owned great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> and some mines, controlled the town councils (cabildos), bought royal offices when the crown sold them, and some families won titles of nobility. By the 18th century, though kept from most of the highest posts, criollos were the wealthy class of landowners, miners, merchants, lawyers, clergy and officers. Most were far humbler. Spaniards were often the largest group in the colonial cities, and many worked as artisans, estate supervisors, shopkeepers and clerks, or lived in poverty. Criollos took pride in their homeland: in 1648 a criollo priest, Miguel Sánchez, published the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a>, widely read as a sign of God’s favour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. They also nursed grievances: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> gave Spanish-born men offices criollos had held, and the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767 exiled their Jesuit sons.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth to Spanish parents made a criollo, but so, in practice, could wealth and a Spanish way of life: the child of a castizo and a Spaniard counted as Spanish, and prosperous families paid to obscure a mixed ancestry. A criollo family that lost its fortune kept its legal status but could sink into poverty.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Spaniards made up about 18–22 per cent of New Spain’s people in its census of 1793, on different authors’ reckonings, nearly all of them born in America.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Criollo people</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">Hacienda</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The clergy <span class="soc-term">clero secular (parish priests), clero regular (friars), monjas (nuns)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Priests, friars and nuns ran the churches, schools and hospitals of Spanish America.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The Catholic Church reached into every town, and its clergy formed an estate of their own. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patronato-real">Patronato real</a> the crown chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, drew the parish boundaries and collected the tithe, a tenth of farm produce, for the Church’s support. Ordained clergy had the fuero eclesiástico, the right to be tried in church courts, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> curtailed it. Parish priests, mostly criollos, drew salaried benefices; clerics without one lived on chantries (capellanías), endowments for Masses for the dead that pious families often entrusted to a son. Religious orders, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, and convents owned estates and city property, and the Jesuits ran the best colleges until the crown expelled them in 1767. Convents took the daughters of families who could pay a large dowry: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> had 22. A church council barred Indigenous men from the priesthood in 1555, and men of African descent were excluded too, while mestizos could be ordained. The Inquisition watched over the faith of everyone except the Indigenous.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Sons of Spanish families, and some mestizos, entered the priesthood through the seminaries and universities; daughters entered convents with a dowry. The highest posts went mostly to men born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> kept criollos further from them.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count for the whole population: in 17th-century Lima, a city of some 26,000, about one person in ten was a priest, friar or nun.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junipero-serra">Junípero Serra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patronato-real">Patronato real</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Indigenous nobles <span class="soc-term">caciques, cacicas, principales (New Spain); kurakas, curacas (Peru)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Indigenous nobles kept their titles under Spanish rule and governed their own towns for the king.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Spanish rule rested on the Indigenous nobility. The crown recognised the old ruling families of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the Andes as nobles so long as they were loyal and Catholic: caciques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, kurakas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> nobles who proved their descent could be recognised as hidalgos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. They bore the titles don and doña, paid no tribute, could ride horses and carry swords or firearms, and some held entailed estates (cacicazgos). They governed the Indigenous towns, collected the tribute and supplied men for the labour drafts. Many prospered as ranchers, traders and muleteers, and some owned enslaved Africans. A cacique family petitioning the viceroy of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1769 listed what it expected: a seat apart from commoners at public functions, freedom from tribute and from jail for debt, and its name among the nobles in official registers. In the 18th century rising royal demands squeezed them between the state and their communities; in 1780 the kuraka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> led the Andean rebellion.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Rank came by descent, proved in genealogies before Spanish courts, but the colonial authorities could also favour a compliant rival over the rightful heir. After the rebellion of 1780–1781 the crown stopped recognising many kurakas and banned the dress and emblems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> nobility.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the caciques, kurakas and their families for either viceroyalty.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>11–15%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Mestizos <span class="soc-term">mestizos, castizos (castas)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Mestizos had both Spanish and Indigenous ancestors, and unlike Indigenous people they paid no tribute to the king.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Mestizos, the descendants of Spaniards and Indigenous people, were the first and the most favoured of the castas, the people of mixed descent. Early on, many were raised by their Spanish fathers; others grew up in their mothers’ communities. The law counted them in the república de españoles. Unlike Indigenous commoners and people of African descent, mestizos paid no tribute, and they could be licensed to carry weapons and, by a grant of Philip II, ordained as priests. They were forbidden to live in Indigenous towns, though some did and even rose to office there. As early as 1543 officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> classed them with vagabonds (vagabundos), and colonial writers held that the “stain” of Indigenous ancestry disappeared in three generations of marriage with Spaniards: a mestizo and a Spaniard had a castizo, a castizo and a Spaniard a Spaniard. In the towns and on the estates mestizos worked as artisans, traders, foremen and labourers.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> An Indigenous villager who learned Spanish, lived as a mestizo and moved to a town could be recorded as one and so escape tribute; a mestizo family that married Spaniards could, in two generations, be written down as Spanish.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Roughly: the census of 1793 counted the castas together at 21–25 per cent of New Spain’s people, on different authors’ reckonings, and about 10 per cent were of African descent, which leaves some 11–15 per cent of Spanish and Indigenous descent.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/encomienda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>At most 57% (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Indigenous commoners <span class="soc-term">indios, macehuales, tributarios</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people were Indigenous farmers who lived in their own towns and paid a yearly tax to the king of Spain.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most people were Indigenous commoners, legally free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the king, grouped in the república de indios. They lived in their own towns, with Indigenous councils (cabildos), community land, churches and confraternities (cofradías), and non-Indians were forbidden to settle among them. Commoners paid the crown a yearly tribute, and their towns owed labour drafts. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, organised by Viceroy Francisco de Toledo from 1573, took one adult man in seven from much of the southern Andes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, and others to the mercury mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huancavelica">Huancavelica</a>, work so feared that many fled their villages. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> the repartimiento drafted smaller numbers for a few weeks a year, for a wage. District officials also forced goods on them at high prices. Yet the law also protected them: they were outside the Inquisition’s reach, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> a General Indian Court, funded by a half-real tax, heard their cases. Communities sued to defend their land and water, and when lawsuits failed they rose in revolt.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Moving to a Spanish town, estate or mine, learning Spanish and passing as a mestizo could free a man from tribute and the labour draft; a few proved noble descent in court and joined the nobility.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other ranks: the census of 1793 counted 51–61 per cent of New Spain’s people as Indigenous, on different authors’ reckonings. This count also takes in the clergy and the Indigenous nobles, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">Encomienda</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-porres" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/martin-de-porres.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>10%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Free people of African descent <span class="soc-term">mulatos, pardos, morenos (castas)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Many people of African descent in Spanish America were free and worked as craftsmen, shopkeepers and soldiers.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">By the 17th century free people of African descent outnumbered the enslaved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and by 1800 they were about one person in ten there. Most were of mixed descent (mulatos or pardos); free Black people were called morenos. They belonged to the república de españoles but bore the stigma colonial ideology attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, which writers held never washed out. They paid tribute like Indigenous commoners but, unlike them, answered to the Inquisition. Laws from 1577 forbade them to carry weapons, and they were barred from the priesthood and from most schools and universities. They worked as artisans, shopkeepers and servants, some bought land, and a few wealthy mulattoes held minor offices. Free men of colour also served in militia companies of their own, which late in the 18th century brought the fuero militar, the only special privilege open to men of mixed descent. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-porres">Martin de Porres</a>, son of a Spanish nobleman and a freed woman, was first admitted to the Dominicans only as a servant.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by purchase, an owner’s grant or birth to a free mother. After that a skilled trade, militia service, wealth and marriage could raise a family, and some descendants were recorded as mestizos or even Spaniards.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 10 per cent of New Spain’s people at the end of the 18th century, more than 370,000 people, nearly all of them free (modern estimates).</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-porres">Martin de Porres</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-peruvians">Afro-Peruvians</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">9</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>0.2%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people <span class="soc-term">esclavos; bozales (born in Africa), ladinos (Spanish-speaking), criollos (born in the colonies)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many saved money to buy their freedom.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">From the 16th century some 200,000 enslaved Africans reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and some 95,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, by modern estimates, many under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">asiento</a> contracts the crown sold to foreign traders. Captives born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> were called bozales, those who spoke Spanish ladinos, and those born in the colonies criollos. They were property, and could be bought, sold, punished and separated from their families. Many lived in the cities as servants, cooks, laundresses and skilled artisans, often hired out; others worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and coastal Peru, in vineyards and in textile workshops (obrajes). In New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> declined after 1640; by 1810 few enslaved people remained. Spanish law, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a> on, left them some rights: to marry in church, to keep savings and to buy their freedom, often in instalments (coartación), and freedom was commoner in towns than in the countryside. Many resisted by running away, changing masters or forming maroon communities (palenques): in 1609 Gaspar Yanga’s maroons near Orizaba won recognition as a free town.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by purchase, often in instalments, by an owner’s grant in life or in a will, or by flight; a child followed its mother’s status, so a free mother’s children were born free.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 10,000 enslaved people in New Spain by 1810, under 0.2 per cent of its people (a modern estimate); Peru, whose coastal estates still bought Africans into the 1800s, had proportionally more, but there is no figure for the two together.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">Asiento</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="spanish-america">Be born in Spanish America</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>A person’s calidad, the standing written into parish books, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> and lawsuits, rested on descent but also on wealth, occupation, legitimacy, dress and reputation, and the same person could appear under different labels in different records. Indigenous villagers who moved to the towns and spoke Spanish could be recorded as mestizos and escape tribute, while a mestizo might pass as an Indian to escape the Inquisition. Prosperous mixed families who married Spaniards could be recorded as Spanish within a few generations, and from 1795 the crown sold a few wealthy pardos dispensations from their colour (the gracias al sacar). Militia service gave men of colour privileges, and the courts were open to ambitious claims: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> sued for the Marquisate of Oropesa, a title held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> descendants, before he rebelled. Many historians now read the casta paintings as an idealised scheme, not daily practice. Independence ended the legal ranking: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> of 1821 made all inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> citizens without distinction, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> abolished titles of nobility in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1825.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women took their family’s calidad, and honour weighed most on Spanish women: the Royal Pragmatic on Marriage of 1776 let parents veto their children’s unequal matches. Spanish law let women own and inherit property, and widows ran estates and businesses. Convents took elite daughters with large dowries, and in 1724 Corpus Christi opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> for noble Indigenous women. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz</a>, an illegitimate criolla, became a nun in 1669 so that she could study, and in 1691 defended women’s right to learning in her Reply to Sister Philotea. Cacicas held titles and estates in their own right. Maya women’s weaving helped pay their households’ tribute, and women of African descent, enslaved and free, worked as cooks, laundresses and servants. Indigenous and enslaved women had little protection from sexual abuse by Spanish men. Micaela Bastidas, of Indigenous and African descent, commanded rebels beside her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> and was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in 1781.</p></section>
